      "q": "6. The art of origami is about enjoying the process.\nClass 7 English Chapter 8 Question Answer – Let us speak (Page 115)\nCreate any craft item using eco-friendly materials. Make a presentation in front of your classmates and teacher about the craft item you have made.\nYour presentation should include the following information:\nIntroduction of the item\nDescription of how you made it\nReason why you like the craft\nConclusion\nUse the phrases given below to make your presentation.\nToday, I’m going to show you a… I made.\nThis… is made of… (materials)\nIt’s… (describe colour, shape, size)\nIt can be used to…\nFirst, I started by…\nThen, I used… (tool or material) to…\nThe most difficult part was to…\nThis… is special to me because…\nI hope you enjoyed seeing my craft and learning about it.\nThank you for your attention.",
      "a": "wer:\n“Today, I want to show you a paper boat that I made. It is crafted from recycled paper taken from an old notebook. The boat is small, white, and shaped like a triangle with neat, sharp edges. It can be used to float on water or simply kept as a decoration. I began by cutting a square sheet of paper and then carefully folded it step by step into the shape of a boat. The trickiest part was forming the sharp edges so it would float properly. This boat is special to me because it reminds me of the poem ‘Paper Boats’ and of my childhood. I hope you enjoyed seeing my craft and learning about it. Thank you for listening.”\nNCERT Class 7 English Poorvi Chapter 8 Question Answer – Let us write (Page 116)\nA diary entry is a personal document. It records an individual’s account of a day of her or his life expressing the thoughts, feelings and opinions in the individual’s mind.\nThe guidelines to compose a diary entry include:\nmentioning day, date and time of writing it\nbeginning with a general sentence describing the day or feelings towards an event or experience\ngiving details\nconcluding with a fial remark\nNow, write a diary entry expressing your joy experiencing a rainy day. You may begin as given below:\nOld Actions\nTime Expressions\nNew Actions\n(i) Rohan packed his suitcase.\nbefore, after, by the time, when\nHe left for the airport.\n(ii) We started work.\nHe arrived in the morning.\n(iii) They set up the tents.\nThe others brought food and medicine.\n(iv) The students became quiet.\nThey heard the teacher coming.\n(v) The villagers left the village.\nThe flood submerged the entire area.\nAnswer:\nFriday, 15 July 2025\n6:30 pm\nDear Diary,\nToday was quite humid, and on my way home from school, I kept hoping it would rain. And it did! The sky grew dark, and soon raindrops began tapping on my umbrella. I couldn’t resist the excitement—I rushed home, took some old paper, and made paper boats just like the ones from the poem we read. I floated them in a puddle outside and watched them glide along with the water. The cool breeze, the sound of the rain, and the little boats drifting away filled me with joy. It felt like a tiny adventure! I hope it rains again tomorrow.\nGood night,\nShaana\nClass 7th English Chapter 8 Paper Boats Question Answer – Let us explore (Pages 116-117)\nI. Shiuli flowers often find a mention in Rabindranath Tagore’s poems. These flowers are found in many parts of our country.\nLook at the pictures of shiuli flowers and recall what you call them in your language.\nAnswer:\nIn Hindi, shiuli flowers are known as “Parijat” or “Harsingar.”\nQuestion 1. Find out names of some more trees or plants in your locality which have fragrant flowers. Write their names in your language and English language. Draw any two flowers and colour them.\nAnswer:\nIn our neighbourhood, some fragrant flowering plants include:\nJasmine (Chameli in Hindi),\nRose (Gulab in Hindi),\nMarigold (Gainda in Hindi).\nIn our area, we have several fragrant flower plants such as Jasmine (Chameli), Rose (Gulab), and Marigold (Gainda).\nAround our locality, fragrant flowers like Jasmine (Chameli), Rose (Gulab), and Marigold (Gainda) can be found.\nII. Solve the riddles given below. Share your answers with your classmates and the teacher.\nQuestion 1. I have water that you cannot drink,\nPeople come to see how in me the Sun sinks.\nI can be rough, I can be calm,\nI hold within myself plenty of charm.\nMy depths tell a never-ending tale.\nWho am I with dolphins and the whale?\nAnswer:\nOcean\nQuestion 2. I sit in stillness, but move with the wind,\nMy surface mirrors the world you are in.\nThe trees around me live happily,\nIn me, you can take a dip daily.\nHome to ducks, frogs, fish, and all,\nCan you guess what I am, after all?\nAnswer:\nLake\nQuestion 3. I gently travel through valleys deep,\nMy soft murmur can make you sleep.\nLittle paper boats float on me in rain,\nThe merry laughter of the kids I gain.\nYou may sit beside me without any fear,\nWho am I who skips along, crystal clear?\nAnswer:\nStream\nQuestion 4. I have a bed, but I do not sleep.\nI have a bank, but no money I keep.\nI carry stories, old and new,\nHigh mountains to the oceans blue.\nI journey far to meet the sea,\nCan you guess, who I can be?\nAnswer:\nRiver\nUnderstand Poetic Imagination in NCERT Class 7 English Chapter 8\nThe poem\nPaper Boats\nby Rabindranath Tagore sparks creative thinking in students. By reading NCERT Solutions Class 7 English Chapter 8, you can experience how imagination turns simple activities into meaningful adventures. Understanding the\ndepth of poetic imagery\nhelps in scoring well in English.\nWith stepwise answers, NCERT Solutions for Class 7 English Paper Boats (2026-27) make\npoetry appreciation easy\n. Emphasizing keywords and poetic devices enhances your exam performance. Regular practice will strengthen your command over\nsummary and theme-based questions\n.\nReview the important ideas and\nsymbolism\nin this poem for quick revision before exams. Focus on keywords like “imagination”, “dreams”, and “nature” to write high-scoring answers. Stay confident and keep revising for the best results!"
