      "q": "10. friend\nII. Study the highlighted word in the following sentence from the text.\n…; and Bangadi too may have to face their wrath.\nWas the initial letter ‘w’ pronounced? There are many words in the English language where certain letters are not pronounced but are spelt. They are called ‘silent letters’.\nFor example: campaign, knowledge, pneumonia, honour, calm.\nGolden Grammar Tips\nSilent letters are letters in a word that are written but not pronounced when you say the word\nExamples:\nk in knight (we say “nite”) b in thumb (we say “thum”) w in write (we say “rite”)\nNow, find more words with silent letters.",
      "a": "wer:\nKnife has a silent “k”,\nwrist has a silent “w”, and\nhonest has a silent “h”.\nSimilarly, listen has a silent “t”,\ndoubt has a silent “b”,\nand castle has a silent “t”.\nOther examples include receipt with a silent “p”,\ncomb with a silent “b”,\ngnome with a silent “g”,\nand subtle with a silent “b”.\nIII. Read the following expressions from the text.\nlaughing stock, last laugh Use the dictionary page given below to find the meanings of the following.\nburst out laughing, don’t make me laugh, laugh until you cry, have a good laugh. ,\nNow, use these expressions in sentences of your own.\nAnswer:\nBurst out laughing – to begin laughing all of a sudden.\nSentence: The joke was so hilarious that I suddenly burst out laughing during class.\nDon’t make me laugh – used when someone says something unbelievable or foolish.\nSentence: When he claimed he could lift 200 pounds, I replied, “Don’t make me laugh!”\nLaugh until you cry – to laugh so intensely that tears start rolling down your face.\nSentence: The movie was so funny that I ended up laughing until tears came out.\nHave a good laugh – to enjoy something because it is funny.\nSentence: We had a wonderful laugh thinking about our mischievous childhood moments.\nGOLDEN GRAMMAR TIPS\nDirect speech shows the exact words spoken by a person. It is usually enclosed in quotation marks (“ ”).\nExample-.\nShe said, “I am going to the market.”\nIndirect speech reports what someone said without using their exact words. Quotation marks are not used, and pronouns, tenses, and time expressions often change.\nExample:\nShe said that she was going to the market.\nIV. Rewrite the following sentences in direct speech to indirect speech. Remember to make the necessary changes."
