      "q": "5. What you have learnt from her",
      "a": "wer:\nBrave Woman: Kalpana Chawla — Rephrased Version\nChildhood: Kalpana Chawla was born on March 17, 1962, in Karnal, Haryana. As a child, she was deeply fascinated by airplanes and outer space. She would often gaze at the sky, imagining herself soaring among the stars.\nEducation and Training: Kalpana pursued her passion by studying aeronautical engineering at Punjab Engineering College. She later moved to the United States for higher studies, where she completed her master’s degree and eventually a Ph.D. in aerospace engineering.\nAchievements: She created history by becoming the first woman of Indian origin to journey into space. Her first spaceflight was in 1997 on the Space Shuttle Columbia. She returned to space in 2003, but tragically, the shuttle disintegrated during re-entry, leading to the loss of Kalpana and her fellow astronauts.\nContributions: Kalpana Chawla showed the world that determination and dedication can turn dreams into reality. Her achievements motivated countless young girls to follow paths in science, engineering, and space research.\nWhat I Learned from Her: Kalpana’s story teaches us to aim high, persevere, and stay committed to our goals. She proved that no aspiration is unreachable when we work with passion and persistence. Her bravery and love for space continue to inspire people everywhere.\nA Homage to Our Brave Soldiers – Class 7 English Solutions\nExploring\nNCERT Solutions Class 7 English Chapter 15 Rani Abbakka (2026-27)\nhelps students understand the incomparable bravery of Indian heroes. These answers capture the spirit of valour, inspiring you to honour the nation’s sacrifices.\nLearning about\nIndia’s highest gallantry awards\nand the stories of war heroes fosters a sense of gratitude and national pride. By reviewing solutions, you develop deeper respect and clarity on important exam concepts.\nPractice\nexercise-based answers\nregularly for high-scoring results in your finals. Focus on key facts and emotional lessons about patriotism to make your preparation strong and meaningful."
